      "source_sequence": 16,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "JUE Insight: Is hospital quality predictive of pandemic deaths? Evidence from US counties",
      "title_cn": "JUE洞察：医院质量能否预测疫情死亡？来自美国县级数据的证据",
      "authors": [
        "Johannes S. Kunz",
        "Carol Propper"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "In the large literature on the spatial-level correlates of COVID-19, the association between quality of hospital care and outcomes has received little attention to date. To examine whether county-level mortality is correlated with measures of hospital performance, we assess daily cumulative deaths and pre-crisis measures of hospital quality, accounting for state fixed-effects and potential confounders. As a measure of quality, we use the pre-pandemic adjusted five-year penalty rates for excess 30-day readmissions following pneumonia admissions for the hospitals accessible to county residents based on ambulance travel patterns. Our adjustment corrects for socio-economic status and down-weighs observations based on small samples. We find that a one-standard-deviation increase in the quality of local hospitals is associated with a 2% lower death rate (relative to the mean of 20 deaths per 10,000 people) one and a half years after the first recorded death.",
      "abstract_cn": "在关于COVID-19空间层面相关因素的大量文献中，医院护理质量与结果之间的关联至今鲜少受到关注。为检验县级死亡率是否与医院绩效指标相关，我们评估了每日累计死亡数和危机前的医院质量指标，并控制了州固定效应和潜在混杂因素。作为质量衡量指标，我们使用了疫情前调整后的肺炎入院后30天内超额再入院五年惩罚率，该指标基于救护车出行模式，针对县级居民可及的医院。我们的调整修正了社会经济状况，并对小样本观测进行了降权。我们发现，当地医院质量每提高一个标准差，在首次记录死亡后一年半时，死亡率（相对于每10,000人中20例死亡的平均值）降低2%。",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jue.2022.103472",
